+
+## Target users
+
+### Primary user segment
+
+**Team facilitators**
+
+- Who they are: people responsible for running weekly team meetings and ensuring agreed actions move forward.
+- Current pain points: action items are buried in transcripts or notes, owners and dates are omitted, and follow-up becomes a manual chase.
+- Primary job: turn a completed weekly meeting into a trusted list of owned, dated actions.
+- Success criteria: leave the meeting workflow confident that every action needing follow-up is explicit and ready for the chosen reminder process.
+
+### Secondary user segment
+
+**Action owners**
+
+- Who they are: team members assigned to complete a meeting action.
+- Current pain points: commitments can be unclear, unrecorded, or discovered only after they are overdue.
+- Primary job: receive a clear commitment with an agreed deadline through a facilitator-controlled process.
+- Success criteria: understand what they own and when it is due without receiving unsolicited autonomous messages.
+
+### Pilot stakeholder segment
+
+**Pilot team leads**
+
+- Who they are: leaders of the five participating teams who need evidence that follow-through is improving.
+- Current pain points: overdue work with no owner is hard to see and hard to correct.
+- Primary job: assess whether the pilot improves action accountability without disrupting team routines.
+- Success criteria: obtain the pilot measures needed to decide whether Pulseboard merits further investment.
+
+## User problems we are solving
+
+1. **Meeting actions vanish after discussion**: commitments embedded in a transcript are easy to overlook.
+   - Current workaround: facilitators manually reread notes or rely on memory.
+   - Cost of inaction: important follow-up is never captured or is captured inconsistently.
+
+2. **Actions lack accountability**: a proposed action may have no owner or due date.
+   - Current workaround: people chase clarification in later meetings or messages.
+   - Cost of inaction: actions become overdue without anyone clearly responsible.
+
+3. **Reminders can amplify bad data**: sending a reminder before a facilitator confirms an action can notify the wrong person or establish the wrong deadline.
+   - Current workaround: facilitators avoid reminders or manually vet every message outside one system.
+   - Cost of inaction: low trust in follow-up and more administrative work.
+
+## Jobs-to-be-done
+
+### Core functional job
+
+**Convert a weekly meeting transcript into confirmed, owned, and dated follow-up actions.**
+
+- **When**: after a weekly team meeting with a transcript available.
+- **With**: the facilitator, the transcript, proposed actions, and the participating team.
+- **Goal**: create a reliable follow-up list that can safely enter a reminder workflow.
+
+### Related jobs
+
+1. **Review proposed commitments**: decide which extracted statements are genuine actions. — During the core job.
+2. **Complete missing accountability details**: assign or confirm an owner and a due date. — During the core job.
+3. **Choose a reminder channel**: decide how confirmed actions should be reminded once pilot evidence supports a channel. — After the core job.
+
+### Emotional and social jobs
+
+- Facilitators want to feel in control of what the team commits to and relieved that follow-up is not being lost.
+- Facilitators want to appear organized and careful, rather than careless with colleagues' commitments.
+- Action owners want to avoid feeling surprised by an unagreed or incorrectly assigned task.
+
+### Key desired outcomes
+
+| Priority | Outcome statement | Current pain level |
+|----------|-------------------|--------------------|
+| 1 | Minimize the number of meeting actions that remain unowned or undated | High |
+| 2 | Minimize the time needed to review proposed actions after a meeting | High |
+| 3 | Reduce the likelihood of a reminder being sent for an unconfirmed action | High |
+| 4 | Increase facilitator confidence that recorded actions match team commitments | Medium |
+| 5 | Optimize reminder-channel fit for the pilot teams | Medium |
+
+### Current job executors
+
+| Current solution | Strengths | Weaknesses |
+|------------------|-----------|------------|
+| Manual meeting notes | Familiar and flexible | Actions are easy to omit; accountability fields are inconsistent |
+| Memory and verbal follow-up | No setup cost | Not durable, measurable, or reliable across a week |
+| Calendar tasks or reminders | Can notify people | Does not extract or validate actions from the meeting transcript |
+
+## Success metrics
+
+### Pilot KPIs
+
+| Category | Metric | Target and timeframe | Baseline and measurement method |
+|----------|--------|----------------------|---------------------------------|
+| User adoption | Proposed action-item review rate | At least 80% over four pilot weeks | Measure reviewed proposals ÷ total proposals, per team and in aggregate |
+| Outcome | Unowned overdue actions | 30% reduction after four pilot weeks | Establish a pre-pilot count for each team; compare the same count at week four |
+| Safety | Reminder eligibility completeness | 100% of reminder-eligible actions have confirmed owner and due date | Audit the reminder-eligible action set throughout the pilot |
+| Learning | Reminder-channel decision | One evidence-backed email-or-Slack decision at pilot review | Record facilitator feedback and observed reminder use by channel |
+
+### Definition of done for the pilot
+
+- The five pilot teams can upload a transcript, review proposed actions, and confirm owners and due dates.
+- Pilot measurement can calculate the four specified KPIs.
+- The confirmation gate prevents a reminder from being scheduled for an action missing an owner or due date.
+- The pilot review can make a channel decision without treating Pulseboard as an autonomous sender.
+- The pilot has enough facilitator feedback to judge whether the workflow reduces unowned overdue actions.
+
+## Business model and commercialization
+
+**Value proposition**: Pulseboard reduces the administrative and accountability loss between a weekly meeting and its follow-up actions.
+
+**Commercial status**: Pricing, buyer, and revenue model are intentionally uncommitted during the five-team pilot. The immediate value test is improved review and accountability, not monetization.
+
+**Business-model validation needed**: Yes. After the pilot establishes value, validate who purchases the product, what value metric supports pricing, and whether the product should remain a standalone B2B SaaS offering.
+
+**Critical assumptions to test**:
+
+1. Facilitators will upload transcripts and review proposed actions as part of their weekly routine.
+   - Validation: review-rate measurement and facilitator feedback during the pilot.
+2. Facilitator confirmation materially reduces unowned overdue actions.
+   - Validation: team-level before-and-after comparison after four weeks.
+3. One reminder channel will fit the pilot teams better than the other.
+   - Validation: structured channel feedback and observed use before any durable integration decision.
+
+## High-level scope
+
+### Core capabilities for v1
+
+**Transcript-to-proposal review**
+
+- What: accept an uploaded transcript and present proposed action items for facilitator review.
+- Why critical: the product's value begins by surfacing commitments that would otherwise be lost.
+- User value: facilitators start from a candidate list rather than reconstructing actions manually.
+
+**Accountability confirmation**
+
+- What: require the facilitator to confirm an owner and a due date for every action that can progress to a reminder.
+- Why critical: this is the safety and trust boundary of the product.
+- User value: follow-up is based on explicit commitments, not inferred or incomplete data.
+
+**Action-status visibility and pilot measurement**
+
+- What: make it possible to see reviewed, confirmed, overdue, and unowned actions and calculate the pilot KPIs.
+- Why critical: the pilot cannot demonstrate value without visibility into these states.
+- User value: facilitators and team leads can see whether meeting commitments are being made accountable.
+
+**Channel-decision support**
+
+- What: capture enough evidence to decide whether the first reminder channel should be email or Slack.
+- Why critical: the brief explicitly leaves this decision open.
+- User value: avoids prematurely building the wrong integration.
+
+### Future capabilities
+
+**Selected reminder delivery**
+
+- What: integrate the chosen reminder channel after pilot evidence supports email or Slack.
+- Why deferred: the correct channel is unknown, and v1 must not turn Pulseboard into an autonomous sender.
+- Prerequisite: pilot channel decision plus a separate product and consent design for delivery.
+
+**Meeting recording and transcription**
+
+- What: capture meetings or generate transcripts.
+- Why deferred: transcripts are inputs to this product; recording is a distinct product surface.
+- Prerequisite: an explicit strategic decision to expand the product boundary.
+
+### Explicitly out of scope
+
+- **Meeting recording**: Pulseboard consumes uploaded transcripts and does not record meetings.
+- **Calendar replacement**: Pulseboard tracks meeting follow-up; it does not schedule or replace calendar workflows.
+- **Autonomous message sending**: no reminder is sent without a facilitator-controlled process and an explicit later channel decision.
+- **Premature email or Slack lock-in**: neither reminder channel is selected by this specification.
+
+## Constraints and requirements
+
+### Business constraints
+
+- **Pilot scope**: five teams.
+- **Success window**: four weeks from pilot start for the stated outcome measure.
+- **Budget and resourcing**: not supplied; subsequent context work must identify them before commitment to an implementation plan.
+- **Commercial and legal conditions**: not supplied; transcript privacy, retention, and any workplace-data obligations require explicit context discovery before implementation.
+
+### Product and technical constraints
+
+- **Platform**: browser-accessible web product.
+- **Input boundary**: an uploaded meeting transcript is the starting input.
+- **Safety invariant**: an action cannot be eligible for reminder scheduling without a facilitator-confirmed owner and due date.
+- **Integration boundary**: email and Slack remain alternatives to be decided after pilot learning.
+- **Scale boundary**: optimize the first version for five pilot teams rather than broad enterprise rollout.
+
+### User-experience constraints
+
+- The review flow must make proposed-versus-confirmed status obvious.
+- Missing owner or due-date information must be clear enough for a facilitator to resolve before scheduling a reminder.
+- The product must not suggest that it records meetings, manages calendars, or sends messages autonomously.
+
+## Project context
+
+### Business context
+
+The immediate opportunity is to test whether a disciplined review gate can convert weekly-meeting discussion into measurably better ownership. The five-team pilot provides a constrained setting in which success is defined by behavior and outcome, not feature count.
+
+### Technical context
+
+This is greenfield work. No existing systems, stack, integrations, or transcript format guarantees were supplied. Those constraints belong in the next context and architecture decisions, not in this project vision.
+
+### Organizational context
+
+Facilitators own the review step, action owners receive clarified commitments, and pilot team leads evaluate the pilot's outcome. No named sponsor, technical lead, or support owner was supplied; these roles must be assigned before implementation starts.
+
+## Risks and mitigations
+
+1. **Low facilitator review completion** — Probability: Medium.
+   - Impact: the pilot cannot reach its adoption target or validate the workflow.
+   - Mitigation: make review status measurable and use pilot feedback to remove workflow friction.
+   - Owner: pilot facilitator for each team.
+
+2. **Poor action proposals from transcripts** — Probability: Medium.
+   - Impact: facilitators may spend more time correcting than they save.
+   - Mitigation: retain facilitator confirmation as the authority and evaluate proposal usefulness during the pilot.
+   - Owner: product team during pilot review.
+
+3. **Reminder-channel mismatch** — Probability: Medium.
+   - Impact: reminders may be ignored or disrupt existing team practice.
+   - Mitigation: do not lock email or Slack before evidence; decide after pilot learning.
+   - Owner: product decision owner before any integration work.
+
+4. **Transcript privacy or retention constraints** — Probability: Medium.
+   - Impact: implementation may be blocked or require material handling changes.
+   - Mitigation: identify workplace-data and retention requirements in project context before implementation.
+   - Owner: designated pilot sponsor and product team.
+
+5. **Metric ambiguity** — Probability: Medium.
+   - Impact: the claimed 30% reduction could be incomparable across teams.
+   - Mitigation: define the baseline counting rule before pilot start and apply it consistently at week four.
+   - Owner: pilot measurement owner.
+
+## Delivery approach
+
+**Approach**: a bounded, feedback-led pilot. The minimum viable product is the transcript upload, proposal review, owner-and-due-date confirmation gate, and pilot measurement needed for five teams.
+
+**Rollout**: onboard the five teams, establish their pre-pilot unowned-overdue baseline, run the workflow through four weeks of weekly meetings, then review outcome and reminder-channel evidence.
+
+**Feedback loops**: quantify review completion and action status, then pair those measures with facilitator feedback at pilot review.
+
+**High-level phases**: this vision deliberately does not define epics. The next project phase will clarify operational constraints and then create the project plan.
+
+## Stakeholders
+
+| Role | Name or group | Interest | Influence |
+|------|---------------|----------|-----------|
+| Pilot facilitators | Five participating team facilitators | Fast, trustworthy meeting follow-up | High |
+| Action owners | Members of the pilot teams | Clear commitments and deadlines | Medium |
+| Pilot team leads | Leads of participating teams | Fewer unowned overdue actions and a sound pilot decision | High |
+| Product team | To be assigned | Deliver and evaluate the bounded pilot | High |
+
+**Communication plan**: review pilot measures and facilitator feedback at the end of the four-week window. Escalate transcript handling, reminder-channel, or baseline-definition questions before implementation rather than resolving them implicitly.
